// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Your own wording — config/texts.jsonc
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  Everything the bot says lives in locales/en.json and locales/de.json. Those
//  files are TRACKED IN GIT: an update ships new ones, so an edit you make
//  there is either overwritten on the next deploy or turns the deploy into a
//  merge conflict.
//
//  This file is yours. It is not tracked, it survives every update, and it only
//  needs to contain the messages you actually want to say differently. The
//  structure mirrors the locale files: copy the key you want to change, keep
//  the nesting, drop everything else.
//
//  An override applies to EVERY language. If you run the bot in German and
//  override "common.noPermission" here, that is what German speakers see too —
//  which is what you want when the point is to say it your way rather than to
//  translate it.
//
//  Placeholders in curly braces are filled in by the bot. Keep the ones you
//  need; a placeholder you delete simply is not shown, and one you invent stays
//  in the text as literal {braces}, which is a visible mistake rather than a
//  silent one.
//
//  Copy this file to config/texts.jsonc to start. An empty object is valid and
//  means "use the shipped wording for everything".
//
{
  // Example — remove or replace. Both of these are real keys.
  //
  // "common": {
  //   "noPermission": "⛔ Sorry, this one is staff only."
  // },
  //
  // "points": {
  //   "footer": "{delta} coins  (you now have {total})"
  // },
  //
  // "games": {
  //   "slots": {
  //     "title": "🎰 Lucky Sevens"
  //   }
  // }
}
